# Enforce LF line endings so byte-for-byte comparisons (notably the
# internal/goldencorpus snapshot tests) are identical across operating systems.
# Without this, git's autocrlf converts text files to CRLF on Windows checkout,
# which diverges from the LF-committed golden fixtures and the LF output the
# formatters produce — breaking the windows-latest CI golden tests.

# Golden snapshot fixtures: always LF, treat as text with fixed eol.
internal/goldencorpus/testdata/golden/** text eol=lf

# Go sources and common text formats: normalize to LF in the repo and on checkout.
*.go    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# CHANGELOG.md: keep both sides' lines when two branches edit the same spot.
#
# Every PR appends its bullet to the end of the same [Unreleased] subsection, and #751 made an entry
# mandatory for any PR that changes what a release ships, so two open PRs nearly always touch the
# same hunk. Git cannot tell that "add bullet A" and "add bullet B" do not actually clash, and
# reports a conflict whose only correct resolution is "keep both". The union driver does that
# automatically: measured on #777's real conflict against main, it merged clean and produced the
# same file a by-hand resolution did, every bullet kept, none duplicated.
#
# Two limits. It applies to local merges and rebases; GitHub's own conflict check and merge button
# may not honour it, in which case the banner still appears, but resolving becomes
# `git merge origin/main` with nothing to edit. And it never reports a conflict: if two branches
# reword the SAME existing line, both versions are kept silently. Bullets are appended far more
# often than edited, so that is the cheaper side — but review the diff of a merge that touched an
# existing entry.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
